# Svelte 5 and SvelteKit Best Practices

Standards for writing Svelte 5 components and SvelteKit applications.

<rule>
name: svelte_best_practices
description: Enforce Svelte 5 and SvelteKit conventions and best practices

actions:
  - type: enforce
    message: |
      ## Svelte 5 Runes

      Always use Svelte 5 runes for reactivity. Never use the legacy `$:` reactive syntax.

      ### Props with `$props()`

      ```svelte
      <script lang="ts">
        interface Props {
          title: string;
          count?: number;
          children?: import('svelte').Snippet;
        }

        let { title, count = 0, children }: Props = $props();
      </script>
      ```

      ### State with `$state()`

      ```svelte
      <script lang="ts">
        let count = $state(0);
        let items = $state<string[]>([]);
      </script>
      ```

      ### Derived values with `$derived()`

      Use `$derived()` for computed values instead of `$:` reactive statements.

      ```svelte
      <script lang="ts">
        let count = $state(0);
        let doubled = $derived(count * 2);
        let isEven = $derived(count % 2 === 0);
      </script>
      ```

      ### Side effects with `$effect()`

      ```svelte
      <script lang="ts">
        let count = $state(0);

        $effect(() => {
          console.log(`Count changed to ${count}`);
          // Cleanup function (optional)
          return () => {
            console.log('Cleaning up');
          };
        });
      </script>
      ```

      ### Complex derived values with `$derived.by()`

      Use `$derived.by()` when you need a function body for complex computations:

      ```svelte
      <script lang="ts">
        let items = $state<{ price: number; quantity: number }[]>([]);

        let total = $derived.by(() => {
          let sum = 0;
          for (const item of items) {
            sum += item.price * item.quantity;
          }
          return sum;
        });
      </script>
      ```

      ### Bindable props with `$bindable()`

      In Svelte 5, props are NOT bindable by default. Use `$bindable()` explicitly:

      ```svelte
      <script lang="ts">
        interface Props {
          value: string;
          open?: boolean;
        }

        let { value = $bindable(), open = $bindable(false) }: Props = $props();
      </script>

      <!-- Parent can now use bind:value and bind:open -->
      ```

      ### Debugging with `$inspect()`

      Use `$inspect()` for reactive debugging (development only):

      ```svelte
      <script lang="ts">
        let count = $state(0);
        let user = $state({ name: 'Alice' });

        // Logs whenever count or user changes
        $inspect(count);
        $inspect(user);

        // With custom handler
        $inspect(count).with(console.trace);
      </script>
      ```

      ### Non-reactive snapshots with `$state.snapshot()`

      Get a plain object from reactive state (useful for logging, external APIs, or serialisation):

      ```svelte
      <script lang="ts">
        let user = $state({ name: 'Alice', scores: [1, 2, 3] });

        function saveToApi() {
          // Get non-proxy plain object
          const data = $state.snapshot(user);
          fetch('/api/user', { body: JSON.stringify(data) });
        }
      </script>
      ```

      ## Deep Reactivity Caveats

      `$state` creates deeply reactive proxies. Be aware of these pitfalls:

      ### Don't destructure reactive state

      ```svelte
      <script lang="ts">
        let user = $state({ name: 'Alice', age: 30 });

        // BAD: loses reactivity
        let { name, age } = user;

        // GOOD: access properties directly
        // use user.name and user.age in template
      </script>
      ```

      ### Extracting values breaks reactivity

      ```svelte
      <script lang="ts">
        let todos = $state([{ done: false, text: 'Learn Svelte' }]);

        // BAD: firstTodo is not reactive
        let firstTodo = todos[0];

        // GOOD: use $derived for extracted values
        let firstTodo = $derived(todos[0]);
      </script>
      ```

      ## Component Structure

      ### File organisation

      1. `<script lang="ts">` block first (always use TypeScript)
      2. Markup (HTML)
      3. `<style>` block last (if needed, prefer Tailwind)

      ### Props interface pattern

      Always define a `Props` interface for component props:

      ```svelte
      <script lang="ts">
        interface Props {
          required: string;
          optional?: number;
        }

        let { required, optional = 42 }: Props = $props();
      </script>
      ```

      ### Snippets over slots

      Prefer snippets (Svelte 5) over slots for composability:

      ```svelte
      <!-- Parent -->
      <Card>
        {#snippet header()}
          <h2>Title</h2>
        {/snippet}
        {#snippet content()}
          <p>Body content</p>
        {/snippet}
      </Card>

      <!-- Card.svelte -->
      <script lang="ts">
        import type { Snippet } from 'svelte';

        interface Props {
          header: Snippet;
          content: Snippet;
        }

        let { header, content }: Props = $props();
      </script>

      <article>
        {@render header()}
        {@render content()}
      </article>
      ```

      ## SvelteKit Conventions

      ### Load functions

      - Use `+page.server.ts` for server-only data (database, secrets)
      - Use `+page.ts` for universal load functions
      - Always type load functions properly

      ```typescript
      // +page.server.ts
      import type { PageServerLoad } from './$types';
      import { error } from '@sveltejs/kit';

      export const load: PageServerLoad = async ({ params }) => {
        const post = await getPost(params.slug);

        if (!post) {
          error(404, 'Post not found');
        }

        return { post };
      };
      ```

      ### Form actions

      Use form actions for mutations, not API endpoints:

      ```typescript
      // +page.server.ts
      import type { Actions } from './$types';
      import { fail } from '@sveltejs/kit';

      export const actions: Actions = {
        default: async ({ request }) => {
          const data = await request.formData();
          const email = data.get('email');

          if (!email) {
            return fail(400, { email, missing: true });
          }

          // Process form...
          return { success: true };
        }
      };
      ```

      ### File naming

      - `+page.svelte` - Page component
      - `+page.ts` - Universal load function
      - `+page.server.ts` - Server-only load function
      - `+layout.svelte` - Layout component
      - `+layout.ts` / `+layout.server.ts` - Layout load functions
      - `+error.svelte` - Error page
      - `+server.ts` - API endpoint
      - `hooks.server.ts` - Server hooks
      - `hooks.client.ts` - Client hooks

      ### Environment variables

      Use the appropriate `$env` module for environment variables:

      ```typescript
      // Server-only secrets (build-time)
      import { DATABASE_URL } from '$env/static/private';

      // Server-only secrets (runtime)
      import { env } from '$env/dynamic/private';

      // Public variables (build-time, can be used in client)
      import { PUBLIC_API_URL } from '$env/static/public';

      // Public variables (runtime)
      import { env } from '$env/dynamic/public';
      ```

      **Rules:**
      - Never import `$env/static/private` or `$env/dynamic/private` in client code
      - Prefix public variables with `PUBLIC_` in your `.env` file
      - Prefer static imports for better tree-shaking

      ### Error handling hooks

      Implement `handleError` for proper error handling:

      ```typescript
      // hooks.server.ts
      import type { HandleServerError } from '@sveltejs/kit';

      export const handleError: HandleServerError = async ({ error, event, status, message }) => {
        const errorId = crypto.randomUUID();

        // Log to your error tracking service
        console.error({ errorId, error, path: event.url.pathname });

        return {
          message: 'An unexpected error occurred',
          errorId
        };
      };
      ```

      ```typescript
      // hooks.client.ts
      import type { HandleClientError } from '@sveltejs/kit';

      export const handleError: HandleClientError = async ({ error, status, message }) => {
        const errorId = crypto.randomUUID();

        // Report to error tracking (e.g., Sentry)
        console.error({ errorId, error });

        return {
          message: 'Something went wrong',
          errorId
        };
      };
      ```

      ### Link preloading

      Use `data-sveltekit-*` attributes for performance:

      ```svelte
      <!-- Preload data on hover (default) -->
      <a href="/blog" data-sveltekit-preload-data="hover">Blog</a>

      <!-- Preload on viewport enter -->
      <a href="/about" data-sveltekit-preload-data="viewport">About</a>

      <!-- Force full page reload -->
      <a href="/external" data-sveltekit-reload>External</a>

      <!-- Prevent scrolling to top -->
      <a href="#section" data-sveltekit-noscroll>Jump to section</a>
      ```

      ## TypeScript

      - Always use `lang="ts"` in script blocks
      - Define interfaces for props, not inline types
      - Use `import type` for type-only imports
      - Leverage `$types` for SvelteKit generated types

      ## Event Handling

      Use the new event attribute syntax (Svelte 5):

      ```svelte
      <!-- Correct: Svelte 5 syntax -->
      <button onclick={() => count++}>Click</button>
      <input oninput={(e) => value = e.currentTarget.value} />

      <!-- Avoid: Legacy syntax -->
      <button on:click={() => count++}>Click</button>
      ```

      ## Accessibility

      - Always include `alt` attributes on images
      - Use semantic HTML elements (`<nav>`, `<main>`, `<article>`, etc.)
      - Ensure interactive elements are keyboard accessible
      - Use ARIA attributes when semantic HTML isn't sufficient

      ```svelte
      <!-- Good -->
      <button onclick={handleClick}>Submit</button>

      <!-- Bad: div with click handler -->
      <div onclick={handleClick}>Submit</div>
      ```

      ## Shared State

      For cross-component state, use `.svelte.ts` files with `$state`:

      ```typescript
      // state.svelte.ts
      export const appState = $state({
        user: null as User | null,
        theme: 'light' as 'light' | 'dark'
      });

      export function setUser(user: User) {
        appState.user = user;
      }
      ```

      ## Performance

      - Use `{#key}` blocks to force re-renders when needed
      - Avoid unnecessary reactivity (not everything needs `$state`)
      - Use `$effect.pre()` for DOM measurements before paint
      - Leverage `{#await}` for async data in templates

      ## Styling

      - Prefer Tailwind CSS classes over `<style>` blocks
      - Use `class:` directive for conditional classes
      - Keep styles scoped when using `<style>` blocks

      ```svelte
      <a
        href="/about"
        class="text-sm hover:underline"
        class:font-bold={isActive}
      >
        About
      </a>
      ```

      ## Checklist

      - [ ] Using `$props()` with typed interface
      - [ ] Using `$state()` for reactive variables
      - [ ] Using `$derived()` or `$derived.by()` for computed values
      - [ ] Using `$bindable()` for two-way bound props
      - [ ] Using new event syntax (`onclick` not `on:click`)
      - [ ] Not destructuring reactive state (preserving proxy)
      - [ ] TypeScript enabled with `lang="ts"`
      - [ ] Semantic HTML and accessibility considered
      - [ ] Load functions properly typed with `$types`
      - [ ] Environment variables using correct `$env` module
      - [ ] Error hooks implemented for production apps

metadata:
  priority: high
  version: 2.0
</rule>
